    {
      "title": "Stitch Patterning",
      "slug": "stitch-patterning",
      "displayOrder": 7,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 562,
        "lessonOrder": 7
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "exerciseAccordion",
              "sections": [
                {
                  "title": "Planning",
                  "bodyHtml": "<style>\r\n\thr.style13 {\r\n\t\theight: 10px;\r\n\t\tborder: 0;\r\n\t\tbox-shadow: 0 10px 10px -10px #8c8b8b inset;\r\n\t}\r\n\r\n</style>\r\n\r\n\t\r\n\t\t<i class=\"fa fa-check\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i><b> Consider Stitch Multiples:</b>\r\n\t\t<ul>\r\n\t\t\t<li>Do you have enough stitches so the pattern can repeat evenly? (including seaming stitches)<br>If not, can you \"fudge\" one or 2 stitches? (add more or less stitches to the cast on)</li><br>\r\n\t\t\t<li>For punchcard knitters: Is the cast on (or section where you are adding your stitch pattern) divisible by 24 to accommodate your punchcard design? (don't forget seaming stitches)</li>\r\n\t\t\t<li>Use knitters graph paper to plan your design</li>\r\n\t\t</ul>\r\n\t\t<div class=\"videopopup\" data-videotitle=\"Stitch Multiples\" data-vimeoid=\"151857799\">\r\n\t\t\t<img class=\"img-thumbnail\" width=\"300\" src=\"https://i.vimeocdn.com/video/637021546-eb939b841562b4b11cd89544115c17fcb7c18b5389273aa2452b3fc6322558f5-d_1280x720?r=pad\">\r\n\t\t\t<br><i class=\"fa fa-youtube-play\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> Video: Stitch Multiples\r\n\t\t</div>\r\n\r\n\t\r\n\t\r\n\r\n\t\t<img class=\"zoom\" width=\"300\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/91/stitch_multiple.jpg\">\r\n\r\n\t\r\n\r\n<hr class=\"style13\">\r\n\r\n\t\r\n\r\n\t\t<i class=\"fa fa-check\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>\r\n\t\t<b>Plan your stitch pattern</b><br>\r\n\t\tIn addition to considering stitch multiples, consider floats.\r\n\r\n\t\tAlthough there are many ways to deal with them, examine your stitch pattern closely.<br>Notice how there are random \"snowflakes\" (single stitches) surrounding the central motif. These will make this snowman easier to knit with smaller floats<br>\r\n\t\r\n\t\t<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/91/snowman.jpg\">\r\n\t\r\n",
                  "iconSrc": "/challenge/images/v2/91/arrow1.png"
                },
                {
                  "title": "Tension",
                  "bodyHtml": "\r\nEven though it's appears to be stockinette, fairisle knits at a very different gauge. <br>\r\nFor a small stocking this difference may not be noticeable, but it is something to consider.<br>If you are planning a fairisle border on a small piece, you might loosen the tension in the fairisle section to prevent pulling in.<br>\r\nfor a fairisle border on a large piece, consider adding stitches for the fairisle section, then decrease these stitches for the plain section.\r\n\t<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/91/2021-09-29_14-38-01.jpg\" width=\"300\"><br>\r\nNotice how the fairisle border distorts the knitting.\r\n\r\n\r\n<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/91/2021-09-29_14-37-18.jpg\">\r\n<br>\r\nStockinette: 25sts x 40 rows = 4\"<br>\r\nFairisle: 29 sts x 37 rows = 4\"\r\n",
                  "iconSrc": "/challenge/images/v2/91/arrow2.png"
                },
                {
                  "title": "Floats",
                  "bodyHtml": "\r\n\r\nFairisle stitch patterns are created by carrying a strand of yarn across the knitting, alternating colors. these strands are called floats.<br>The more stitches between the color changes, the longer the floats.<br>Watch as we explore 10 ways to manage floats.\r\n\r\n <div class=\"videopopup\" data-videotitle=\"Managing Floats\" data-vimeoid=\"475492261\">\r\n\t\t<img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"https://i.vimeocdn.com/video/988244244-1801b21cd167065fd204fa0491fdf02bcf768a71dd510e2cecfcd39a0d1a1cc6-d_1280x720?r=pad\"><br><center><i class=\"fa fa-youtube-play\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> 10 ways to manage floats</center>\r\n\t\t<div class=\"imagecenter text-center\"><button class=\"btn btn-sm\"><i class=\"fa fa-youtube-play fa-2x learn\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></button></div>\t\t\r\n</div>\r\n",
                  "iconSrc": "/challenge/images/v2/91/arrow3.png"
                }
              ],
              "legacy": {
                "showText": "below",
                "size": null
              },
              "legacyComponentId": 6622,
              "order": 2
            }
          ],
